  "description": "Strongly-typed Avalonia theming. Decorate a class with [Theme], install this package, and the bundled Roslyn source generator emits one markup extension per public property, so your XAML binds theme values directly with full IntelliSense and no runtime code generation. Extensions sort into three namespaces by the property's C# type: {colour:...} for paint (Avalonia.Media.Color and anything implementing IBrush), {size:...} for measures (double/int/float/decimal, Thickness, CornerRadius, GridLength, Point, Size) and {theme:...} for everything else. Breaking change in 1.11.0: colour and size tokens moved out of {theme:...}; add the two xmlns and repoint those usages.\n\nACCENTS. {colour:AccentBrush} resolves against whichever hue the target control carries, so one accent style block serves every hue instead of a copy per accent. Callers write theme:Accent.Kind=\"Warning\"; the hue inherits, so it reaches control template parts and a container tints everything beneath it. Hues and shades are inferred from the theme's own Color Accent{Hue} and Accent{Hue}{Shade}Brush properties, so adding a hue is a theme edit and nothing else: the enum, the XAML value and the IntelliSense follow. Hues added downstream are honoured by control styles compiled before they existed.\n\nICONS. Mark a class [IconGallery] and every public string property becomes an {icons:IconName} extension.\n\nSTYLE CLASSES. A bundled MSBuild target generates the declarations that stop IDEs underlining Classes=\"accent\" on markup that works, keeping them in step with your ControlThemes automatically.\n\nIncludes AngelSix.ThemeEngine.SourceGen as an analyzer; a single install gives you the runtime, the generator and the build targets.",
